config BR2_PACKAGE_CADR_TERMINAL
	bool "cadr-terminal"
	select BR2_PACKAGE_CADR_COMMON
	help
	  The screen, the keyboard and the mouse: the program on the
	  processing system that shows the CADR's display to a VNC viewer
	  and carries the viewer's keys and pointer back to the machine.
	  The display board keeps no frame buffer in the fabric --- the
	  picture is written into the display's own region of DDR --- so
	  this maps that region and serves it over RFB.

	  A key becomes one of MIT's own key positions through muir's own
	  mapping, and a pointer becomes counts on the mouse's quadrature
	  lines, both through the I/O board's cables on M_AXI_GP0.  On a
	  bitstream without those cables, and under --no-input, the screen
	  is served read-only and a viewer's input is counted and dropped.

	  It has an init script, as cadr-disk-pack does and unlike
	  cadr-console: a viewer connects when somebody wants to look, and
	  nothing is on the bus while nobody does.
